d however for  most of our our users um  we wind up going more with the thermo  approach and  and honestly one of the big reasons is  cost and the other reason  is the ability to look at a large  number of cells so you can literally  take  millions and millions of t cells out of  peripheral blood and  apply the thermo approach and get you  know a large number of the clones  identified  in your sample whereas with single cell  rna sequencing  even um you know with with modern  techniques with hashtagging  you're only looking at a few thousand  cells at a time so it really does become  a trade-off  of do you really need to know the entire  sequence for  your energy receptor versus do you want  to look at  a large number of cells in a population  for questions of clinical research  where you need to look at lots of  numbers of samples  thermo's approach really does become a  really powerful tool  because you can look at lots of cells  and because of the lower cost  for a lot of our more basic science  types of questions that's where we see  more use of the of the single cell  thank you dr o'connor can you talk more  about  how you would use tcr seek in your  analysis for sarcoidosis disease  progression  specifically in terms of phenotyping  disease course that gets worse  versus gets better  yeah that's that's one of our favorite  questions and it's the one that um that  we think about a lot  so so again with sarcoidosis  it's a disease of unknown ideology and  it's variable disease course  and also we see that a lot in in  autoimmune type or inflammatory type  chronic  diseases so everybody would love to have  some  biomarkers or some handle that within  the immune system that would help us  understand  why there's differential progression or  differential disease phenotypes  and ultimately why disease course looks  so different  so we've been tracking t cells and b  cells  in sarcoidosis through things like rna  sequencing  my lab specifically interested in  epigenetics so we  look at histone modifications by chip  sequencing and through those methods  we've been able to at a bulk population  level  understand some of the molecular  programming that's happening inside of  the cell so  whether a cell is it seems to be  programmed more towards  a th1 type of function or a t17 type of  function  and then we try to relate that to to  what's happening  in terms of disease course but none of  that type of analysis  rnac or chipseek none of that type of  analysis really gives us a handle on  individual clones or individual t cells  and what's happening within the t cell  population and because we know that  a hallmark of adaptive immunity is this  idea of clonal expansion the the  observation that  when an immune response occurs you see  this expansion of a specific clone which  then translates into a specific  unique combination of t cell receptor or  b cell receptor genes  being able to observe that  expansion of those t cell receptor and  bc b cell receptor genes  allows us to understand what's happening  actively in  those two compartments and so what we're  doing now with t-cell receptor and  b-cell receptor sequencing in  sarcoidosis is trying to understand  what is happening in b-cells and t cells  over time and how does that correlate  with some of these differential  molecular programming that we're seeing  so some of the questions we're trying to  ask are do we see  changes in tcr genes being used  essentially are we seeing different  kinds of t-cell clones  associated with different t cell  functions so  are we seeing examples of of clones that  seem to be associated more  with th1 function or more with th17  function  are we seeing specific clones arising  that seem to be associated with  different disease progression and i  think some of the evidence i showed you  today is that  at least early on in very primary  analysis we are seeing  signs that specific clones are  do seem to be associated with with  progressive disease  or at least active disease so the way  that we're thinking about using tcr  is applying it in the context of these  other assays using  acids such as rna-seq or  or chipseek to understand the molecular  underpinnings of the function  that seems to be associated with  worsening disease but then  understanding does that seem to be  associated with specific t cell  populations through this tcr analysis  we're doing something similar with with  b  cells we do see evidence of a specific b  cell function that seems to be  associated with  sarcoidosis and so again we'd like to  understand is that is that  broad across different kinds of b cells  or does it seem to be associated with  specific types of b cells  and that's that's um that's a line of  research that we're just starting to get  into  so we think of tcr and bcr sequencing as  really a nice way to to sort of reach  into the immune  compartment and try to identify specific  activity at an individual clone level  thank you let's go with this next  question what kind of dedicated  bioinformatic resources  do you need to perform this data  analysis  yeah so um i hope that  i hope that i was able to give everyone  the idea that this  this is a really easy system to use um  again i want to say i'm not  trained in bioinformatics or  computational biology and  um all of the data figures or a lot of  them anyway that you saw today i  generated  and that says a lot so we  we have we have really great  bioinformatics resources and  computational  biology researchers here at national  jewish  we have a whole dedicated um facility  for bioinformatics we have a super  computer cluster  on campus so we have really uh really  you know beefy  capability and um you you really don't  need it  um to do this kind of analysis thermo  fisher has provided a really amazing  very intuitive very user-friendly tool  that that is fairly seamless  with with the assay we have  we have done some um tcr sequencing  for individuals outside of national  jewish and have been able to interface  with them through the tool  and they found it very easy to use um  very intuitive  so so really you know if you're a lab  that doesn't have access to lots of  bioinformatic  tools or or individuals and you don't  really know anybody who's  a computational biologist you can still  do this  type of analysis um therma's really  great at providing you a walkthrough  with  the online tools and what's available so  so i really do want to encourage  everybody  if you know if you don't feel  comfortable  in the computational space it's this  that's really not a  barrier to do doing this kind of  analysis  can you compare and contrast the use of  dna  versus dna rna for the assay  yeah um so  so we've had i have to say most of what  we've done thus far has been using rna  as our input and we've had really great  luck  going down to very low amounts of input  and  generally in the context of clinical  research you're taking your samples and  trying to do lots of different things  with them so  it really fits in well in that context  so you know we typically  are taking our samples and trying to  pull off um you know bulk rna-seq  and maybe chip seek and so being able to  not  being able to use a low amount of  material  going into this analysis is is one of  the huge  advantages that we that we find so  that's that's on the rna side  we have had a couple of examples and  again you know i think a really good  example of this  would be somebody who's sitting on a lot  of ffte samples  um those samples tend to be when you  isolate the nucleic acid  tends to be really degraded uh material  and and  you know usually for next gen sequencing  that's that's really difficult to deal  with  the ampliseq system handles um degraded  material really well  again because it's it's this combination  of pcr and  sequencing as the readout so  so it handles that really well and  that's really a context where you'd want  to use dna  and that's what we've done in the past  and again it's worked really well in  those cases  with dna you need more input material  but again you know if you're dealing  with or if you have  ffpe samples usually that's not that's  not a problem  so so those would be two different use  cases i know that  some of the other use cases would be if  you are specifically interested in  in cancer and um you know maybe you're  maybe working on tills tumor  infiltrating lymphocytes and you want to  look at  um at frequencies of different types of  t  cells or maybe you're looking at  hematological malignancies and you want  to look at frequencies  that's really again where you want to  use dna there's really not a great way  to use  rna for for deconvolution of that data  so  there's different use cases that really  i think does come down to what your  what your specific questions are  dr o'connor you talked a little bit  about infection-driven  exacerbation in respiratory disease is  that a big  problem and how could you use ngs-based  approaches to address that issue  yeah really good question so so for a  lot of the diseases that we see in  national jewish these respiratory  diseases  um one of the the huge problems that  that we all face are these exacerbation  periods that lead to  to worsening lung function and a lot of  times  these exacerbations are driven by by  infections  so in the case of cf where  they're really strong robust infections  they're fairly easy to see  quote unquote and so doing tcr bcr  sequencing allows you to see the immune  response so essentially track the  infection by tracking the immune  response to that infection  in other cases we we are dealing with  exacerbations where there's suspected  infection but it's really difficult to  track  and so so that's a case again where  ampliseq works really well  so we um we had a a project that was  funded by um the bill melinda gates  foundation  um looking at mtb tuberculosis  and we were able to use an amplistique  approach  to track the presence of tb  in different samples over a treatment  course  and and we tried to do this with other  typical methods such as 16s  qpcr and really the ampliseq approach  again because it's that combination of  pcr and sequencing  amplified the signal to a point where we  were able to get better resolution or at  least  equal resolution to what we were able to  get with 16 sq bcr  but with the huge advantage that now we  could track lots of different  um gene expressions specific to to tb  which allowed us to ask a lot more  questions about what was happening  to the to the pathogen in the context of  the disease course  so um so there's lots of different you  know the power of ampliseq  and and the approach that you can take  with ngs  to ask questions about how do infections  contribute to  uh to disease and disease course over  time  the resolution you get with ngs is  really opens up a lot of new types of  questions  thank you dr o'connor now we have time  for a few more questions  but i do want to let our audience know  that any questions we are unable to  answer  live today and additionally those that  are submitted during the on-demand  period  they will be addressed via the email  address you provided  at the time of registration so we have  time for a few more  let's go with this one why would someone  choose the short read  versus long read version of the essay  yeah um other good questions we didn't  get a lot of time to talk about it but  yeah there are  there is a short read versus a  long-range version of for both tcr  and vcr sequencing so um the short read  uh version again would be great in a  situation of ffte  where you suspect that you're going to  have a lot of degraded  sample and you're not sure about how  much of the intact  gene you're going to have gene  expression you're going to have  or dna intact dna recombination that  you're going to have so the short read  assay would be a really great  application for that  in contrast if you are a b-cell  immunologist or  if you're dealing with a clinical  research project that  has a b-cell component to it and you're  interested in looking at class switch  recombination  so the isotype switching that's really  where you're going to want to look at  the long read assay because you're going  to get the entire  entire vdj segment plus the constant  region that is type switching  so again you know they're really they're  two nice options to have  and it really does come down to what's  the material that you're working with  what's your question  um how much information you're looking  for if you really just  are trying to track clones um the clonal  expansion  observe them then you probably could get  you know use the short read assay  if you want to understand more about the  immunobiology such as cluster  combination then the long-rate assay is  a really good  option for you  thank you would you be expanding on  how you use different tools to examine  molecular programming of immune cells  in the context of respiratory disease  course  specifically in terms of ngs approaches  such as tcrc  or rnac  yeah so um so you know i think everybody  these days is doing some sort of ngs in  their  in their uh in their research and um  we've been involved in different ngs  essays now for for quite a while  one of the things that we are starting  to understand  is that a lot of these assays really do  feed off of each other so if you do  something like rna-seq and chip seek  we start to think of chip-seq looking at  histone modifications is the programming  and rna-seq is the readout of that  programming through transcription  so those two acids can really feed off  of each other and allow you to ask some  really interesting questions  of immune cells in the context of  different diseases  so tcrc and bcrc you know come along  and add another component to to those  kinds of assays  again we we are using things like  chipseek and rnac  to try to understand the molecular  programming  in the context of chronic diseases over  time in these  in these memory cells but understanding  what's happening to the individual cells  the clonal cells the individual  t cells or b cells that express a unique  b-cell receptor t-cell receptor  gives us another layer on top of  understanding that  that programming because if we see  programming changes in the context  of of a disease course  it'd be great to understand if the t  cell compartment and the diesel  compartment is changing as well  or if it's this the same kind of cells  and they're changing over time those are  these are those gives you two very  different scenarios  and gives us a really powerful way to  start thinking about what's happening to  these chronic diseases  and ways that we might be able to think  about  intervening in those inflammatory  courses you dr o'connor and we have time  for one more question  and i couldn't end this webinar without  touching on the subject  as we all know sars kobe 2 is  overwhelming the research community  currently  how do you think it will affect  immunology and disease research  approaches  and how can we use ngs based approaches  to help study the impact  of the virus  yeah that's a that's obviously a really  poignant question  and you know i think uh thermo  therma has come out with an an  amplistique tool  that allows people to sequence to  sequence  the stars cov2 genome um  tools like tcr pcr sequencing you know  obviously allow you to track the immune  response  so you know i don't think any of us have  a really good sense of  how the presence of the virus in the  context of these chronic diseases what  you know what's going to happen  in terms of disease course but obviously  having these tools being able to  understand how the virus  is changing with an individual through  perhaps sequencing  the viral genome with ample seek or  understanding how the chief of  compartment and peaceful department is  changing  in the context of the presence or  absence of virus obviously  those are enormously helpful tools to  understand  what how are we going to have to change  the way that we think  about the way that we conduct our  research projects um  for for these different diseases um so  just just being able to see  those changes um obviously that's kind  of like the first step and  trying to understand how we how we move  forward  thank you dr o'connor and thank you for  this informative q a session  before we go i do want to thank our  audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  there were quite a few questions we were  unable to get to  and additionally those questions that  come in during the on-demand period  we will make sure that these are  addressed via the email address that you  provide  at the time of registration i would once  again like to thank dr brian o'connor  for his time today  and his important research we'd also  like to thank labruts  and our sponsor thermofisher scientific  for underwriting today's  educational webcast today's webcast can  be viewed on demand  and lab roots will alert you via email  when it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  that's all for now we hope to see you  again soon  have a great day
 

